### Tonal colour examples

For a sensitive message, use colours from one tonal range, so that the post feels serious, informative and practical. For example, use Green tint 95% with Green shade 50%, or the reds of a warning.

<%= colour_example('social-colour-tonal', layout: :tiles) %>

### Companion colour set

Use companion colours for emphasis, for example to prompt an action or to share good news. GOV.UK keeps the set to these 9 pairings to help keep them accessible. Accent blue on Primary blue and Primary blue on Primary yellow reach only 3:1, so use them only for large text or graphics, not for smaller text.

- Some pairings are harder to see for people with a visual impairment or colour blindness, so choose carefully.
- Check every pairing that carries text against WCAG 2.2 contrast (minimum), level AA.
- Do not rely on colour alone to show meaning, ask for an action or prompt a response.
- Some people browse in dark mode or with high contrast settings. Check the post there, and adjust the colours if you need to.

